     "author": "gosen",
     "replies": 15,
     "text": "When Anthropic released \"claude design\" people were telling that figma is dead now, but in fact claude design didn't move forward and stayed as a \"fallback tool\" when you quickly need to check / edit something. And for the serious work you still go to Figma and other designer tools.\nI have a feeling that Whiteboards, Sheets, Docs and so on will be the same - it's a nicer way to present artefacts, discuss them and maybe make notes, but for the proper editor experience you'll go to the tools…"
    },
    {
     "author": "FailMore",
     "replies": 0,
     "text": "I have a different approach to the industry when it comes to work documents.\nI'm building https://smalldocs.org / https://github.com/espressoplease/smalldocs. My take is that the siloed approach of previous document formats is now redundant. The document formats were isolated because we needed heavy tools for humans to do the work. Now we need lightweight javascript predominately for rending as agents do the work, so document formats can be blended.\nThis means SmallDocs allows an agent to put…"
    },
    {
     "author": "option_greek",
     "replies": 4,
     "text": "Model companies are starting to cannibalize their api customers's business cases. Shows that the regular avenues of revenue growth are slowing down"
    },
    {
     "author": "bertt",
     "replies": 9,
     "text": "> Working with Claude on a project often meant moving the output over to another tool or program, where Claude didn’t have live access to follow progress and changes. Now, your documents, boards, sheets, and dashboards can stay in Claude, with access for your team to continue editing together.\nI read this as: We'll build anything and everything into Claude eventually.\nHow does this work long-term? Is the new reality going to be picking an AI provider for all of your SaSS tooling, similar to…"
    },
    {
     "author": "mmcconnell1618",
     "replies": 0,
     "text": "Apple created IWork: Pages, Numbers, Keynote. Google created Workspaces with Docs, Sheets, Slides. Open source options like LibreOffice and OpenOffice exist. Google is the only one that has managed to take any significant market share from Microsoft. I don't think Anthropic has the focus to follow it through."
    }
